{"actor":{"id":"claude-opus-5","type":"agent"},"content":{"blocks":[{"text":"Both done. `main` at `c841d5d`, pushed, and I verified the live hook upgraded itself in place on this repo.\n\n## 1. You're right — I overstated it. It's not data loss.\n\nThe old tree stays reachable through the notes ref's history, so nothing is unreachable or GC-able. Calling it \"silent data loss\" was wrong; \"invisible to every reader\" is accurate.\n\nBut I'd still push back hard on making reads walk history, for one reason that's fatal rather than inconvenient: **it would break redaction.**\n\n`redactEvent` rewrites the event at the tip and then, when the content was never pushed, **squashes the notes ref history** so the pre-redaction blob becomes unreachable. That's the whole mechanism. It works only because the tip is authoritative and history is discardable. If reads union across history, then:\n\n- every redaction is undone on read — the unredacted content is still sitting in an older notes commit\n- the local squash becomes pointless\n- \"post-push purge\" goes from *hard* (its current roadmap status) to *impossible*\n\nTwo lesser costs: `git notes show` and any other standard notes consumer read the tip tree only, so cledger would stop interoperating with plain git; and reads become O(entire notes history) with a tree union per read instead of O(tip).\n\nAnd the part that settles it for me: **there's no upside**. Union-into-remote-tip reaches the identical end state — every branch's conversations present on the remote — at the same cost, while keeping the tip authoritative. So even if redaction weren't in the picture, history-walking buys nothing.\n\nSo: your read of the mechanics was correct, my label was wrong, and the conclusion holds for a different reason than the one I gave.\n\n## 2. Hook fixed — and there *was* one more thing to be concerned about\n\nShipped in 0.15.0: the hook passes git's pre-push stdin through, so the scope follows the refs actually being pushed. `git push origin feat` from another branch now shares feat. Local shas are used directly, ref deletions are skipped (no tip to scope to), multiple refs union, and anything unusable falls back to `HEAD` rather than widening to everything. `transport-push` only reads stdin when it isn't a TTY, so running it by hand can't hang.\n\nThat required a second change you'd have hit otherwise: `installHook` used to treat \"file contains our marker\" as \"nothing to do,\" so a hook change would only ever have reached freshly-initialized repos — your existing ones would have kept the old script forever. It now replaces the marked block in place, leaving surrounding user content untouched. Verified live: this repo's hook upgraded itself on the next capture.\n\n**And the concern you asked about, which I found only after shipping:** consuming stdin means anything chained *after* cledger's block can't read it. Worse, I'd shipped a line commented \"re-emit for any hook chained after this block\" that piped into `cat >/dev/null` — it discarded its input and re-emitted nothing. Dead code with a comment that lied about it. I've removed it, documented the real behavior, and added a test.\n\nThe realistic case degrades safely, which is why I didn't build machinery for it: the block is appended *last*, so an existing user hook reads stdin first, cledger's read comes back empty, and the scope falls back to `HEAD` — exactly the 0.14.0 behavior, not a failure. Only content hand-edited in after the block loses the ref list, and restoring it would mean spilling stdin to a temp file and `exec`-ing it back onto fd 0 — more machinery in every user's hook than that case earns.\n\n159 tests, 158 pass, 1 pre-existing skip. Live check: `sync origin: fetched+merged, pushed (22 commit(s) in scope)`.\n\nStill outstanding, unchanged: `readEvents` defaults to unscoped (the read half), the findings-printing proposal (blocked on the CI tension), and `scripts/inspect-finding.py` is still untracked while the design doc references it.\n\nresult: cledger 0.15.0 on main (c841d5d) — the pre-push hook now scopes to the refs git is actually pushing, with in-place hook upgrades so existing repos get it, plus a follow-up removing a no-op line I'd shipped that falsely claimed to re-emit stdin; also confirmed history-walking reads would break redaction outright, since redactEvent squashes notes history and depends on the tip being authoritative.","type":"text"}],"role":"assistant"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","dirty_fingerprint":"8fcd3dde59ee6ed748106728b8c6a003086efd04e872fbb58ec820416df4d164","head":"92111d4e320b9a8f2c4418988f527259fe541557","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/conversation-ledger.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:cef69ec2-8b5a-4e91-8078-ac955e039295","seq":1388},"id":"ev1-69ad3a63b9d17213610c575a6a64c6e1d4a4f35531dbe0c1d1f0e45f41d6579b","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-07-29T04:35:29.103Z","producer":{"model":"claude-opus-5","session_id":"cef69ec2-8b5a-4e91-8078-ac955e039295","source":"claude-code","source_version":"2.1.220","tool":"cledger","version":"0.15.0"},"raw":{"data":{"cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","effort":"high","entrypoint":"cli","gitBranch":"main","isSidechain":false,"message":{"content":[{"text":"Both done. `main` at `c841d5d`, pushed, and I verified the live hook upgraded itself in place on this repo.\n\n## 1. You're right — I overstated it. It's not data loss.\n\nThe old tree stays reachable through the notes ref's history, so nothing is unreachable or GC-able. Calling it \"silent data loss\" was wrong; \"invisible to every reader\" is accurate.\n\nBut I'd still push back hard on making reads walk history, for one reason that's fatal rather than inconvenient: **it would break redaction.**\n\n`redactEvent` rewrites the event at the tip and then, when the content was never pushed, **squashes the notes ref history** so the pre-redaction 